  "intro": "The dot product turns two vectors into a single number. In component form $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=u_1v_1+u_2v_2$ (and $u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3$ in $3$D). Geometrically $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=\\|\\vec{u}\\|\\,\\|\\vec{v}\\|\\cos\\theta$, which lets you find the angle between two vectors, test orthogonality (the dot product is $0$), and project one vector onto another. Throughout, give exact values with radicals, $\\pi$, and fractions unless an approximation is requested.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Given $\\vec{u}=\\langle 3,-4\\rangle$ and $\\vec{v}=\\langle 5,2\\rangle$, find $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The dot product multiplies matching components and adds: $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=u_1v_1+u_2v_2$.",
          "Substitute: $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=(3)(5)+(-4)(2)$.",
          "Compute each product: $(3)(5)=15$ and $(-4)(2)=-8$.",
          "Add: $15+(-8)=7$. The result is a single number (a scalar), not a vector."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=7$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Find the angle $\\theta$ between $\\vec{u}=\\langle 1,\\sqrt{3}\\rangle$ and $\\vec{v}=\\langle \\sqrt{3},1\\rangle$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Use $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}}{\\|\\vec{u}\\|\\,\\|\\vec{v}\\|}$.",
          "Dot product: $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=(1)(\\sqrt{3})+(\\sqrt{3})(1)=2\\sqrt{3}$.",
          "Magnitudes: $\\|\\vec{u}\\|=\\sqrt{1^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2}=\\sqrt{1+3}=2$ and $\\|\\vec{v}\\|=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^2+1^2}=2$.",
          "So $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{(2)(2)}=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{4}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.",
          "The angle whose cosine is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Determine whether $\\vec{u}=\\langle 4,6\\rangle$ and $\\vec{v}=\\langle 3,-2\\rangle$ are orthogonal.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Two nonzero vectors are orthogonal exactly when their dot product is $0$, since $\\cos\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}=0$.",
          "Compute: $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=(4)(3)+(6)(-2)$.",
          "Evaluate: $(4)(3)=12$ and $(6)(-2)=-12$, so $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=12-12=0$.",
          "Because the dot product is $0$, the vectors are orthogonal (perpendicular)."
        ],
        "answer": "Yes; $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=0$, so they are orthogonal."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "Find the vector projection of $\\vec{u}=\\langle 3,4\\rangle$ onto $\\vec{v}=\\langle 2,1\\rangle$, that is $\\operatorname{proj}_{\\vec{v}}\\vec{u}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Use $\\operatorname{proj}_{\\vec{v}}\\vec{u}=\\dfrac{\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}}{\\vec{v}\\cdot\\vec{v}}\\,\\vec{v}$. The denominator is $\\vec{v}\\cdot\\vec{v}=\\|\\vec{v}\\|^2$, not $\\|\\vec{v}\\|$.",
          "Numerator: $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=(3)(2)+(4)(1)=6+4=10$.",
          "Denominator: $\\vec{v}\\cdot\\vec{v}=(2)(2)+(1)(1)=4+1=5$.",
          "Scalar coefficient: $\\dfrac{\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}}{\\vec{v}\\cdot\\vec{v}}=\\dfrac{10}{5}=2$.",
          "Scale $\\vec{v}$: $\\operatorname{proj}_{\\vec{v}}\\vec{u}=2\\langle 2,1\\rangle=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\operatorname{proj}_{\\vec{v}}\\vec{u}=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 5,
      "prompt": "A constant force $\\vec{F}=\\langle 6,2\\rangle$ (in newtons) moves an object along the displacement $\\vec{d}=\\langle 4,1\\rangle$ (in meters). Find the work done.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Work done by a constant force is $W=\\vec{F}\\cdot\\vec{d}$.",
          "Compute the dot product: $W=(6)(4)+(2)(1)$.",
          "Evaluate: $(6)(4)=24$ and $(2)(1)=2$, so $W=24+2=26$.",
          "The units are newton-meters, or joules."
        ],
        "answer": "$W=26$ joules"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 6,
      "prompt": "Find $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}$ for $\\vec{u}=\\langle -2,5\\rangle$ and $\\vec{v}=\\langle 4,3\\rangle$.",
      "hint": "Multiply matching components and add: $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=u_1v_1+u_2v_2$. The answer is one number, not a vector.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Apply $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=u_1v_1+u_2v_2$: $(-2)(4)+(5)(3)$.",
          "Evaluate: $(-2)(4)=-8$ and $(5)(3)=15$.",
          "Add: $-8+15=7$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=7$"
      }
